Section 25 says the state needs a law to limit a right. Section 26 says what that law must look like.

Step one: if the constitution sets specific conditions, follow them. Section 34, for example, allows limits on expression only to protect state security, other people's rights, public order or good morals, or public health. No other reason will do.

Step two: where the constitution sets no specific conditions, the law must still pass a general checkpoint:
- it must not conflict with the rule of law;
- it must not burden or restrict rights more than the reason requires (proportionality: match the hammer to the nail);
- it must not affect human dignity, which section 4 already protects;
- it must state its reasons for the restriction.

Step three: the law must apply generally. It may not be aimed at one particular case or one particular person. The logic is simple. A referee may announce “no shooting from outside the box”. A referee may not announce “the away team's number 10 may not score”. A rule aimed at one person is not a rule at all; it is a verdict that skipped the courtroom.

The exception to know: the last paragraph of section 37 says an expropriation law that names specific land or owners, as necessary, does not breach section 26, paragraph 2. You cannot build one particular road without naming the plots it crosses.

Compared with the previous charter: the 2007 constitution said a rights-limiting law could not affect “the essence” of the right. Section 26 of the 2017 text does not use that phrase. It uses the checklist above instead: rule of law, proportionality, human dignity. If an exam asks for the wording of section 26, go by the current text.

Read through Chapter 3 and one sentence keeps returning: “A person has the liberty to … ; restricting it is not permitted, except …”. What changes is the wording after “except”, and it comes in three strengths.

Strength one: a court order or warrant, or another ground provided by law. This guards the three most intimate spaces: the body (arrest and detention, section 28), the home (entry and search, section 33) and private communication (inspecting, seizing or disclosing what people send each other, section 36). Intrusion into these spaces does damage instantly and cannot be undone, so the default is that a neutral judge looks first. “Another ground provided by law” covers situations such as an offence committed in plain sight, where there is no time to get a warrant.

Strength two: restriction by law, but only for reasons the constitution lists. Expression (section 34), movement and residence (section 38), occupation (section 40), association (section 42) and assembly (section 44) work this way. Parliament can restrict them, but must rely on a listed reason. Movement, for example, can be restricted for state security, public order, town planning, family status or the welfare of minors.

Strength three: absolute, with no “except”, or only a very narrow one:
- torture, cruelty, or inhuman punishment (section 28, paragraph 3);
- exiling a Thai national, barring a Thai national from entering the country, or revoking the nationality of someone who is Thai by birth (section 39);
- closing a news outlet in order to curb its freedom (section 35, paragraph 2);
- forced labour, allowed only for public disasters, a declared emergency, martial law, or war (section 30).

The rule that ties the chapter together: the harder the harm is to reverse, the higher the gate.

Section 34 protects expression: speaking, writing, printing, publishing and every other way of conveying meaning. A law may restrict it only for four purposes: state security, the rights and freedoms of others, public order or good morals, and public health.

The second paragraph protects academic freedom, with a condition exam writers like to test. Academic freedom must not conflict with the duties of the Thai people or with good morals, and must “respect and not obstruct the differing opinions of others”. That last clause ties a scholar's freedom to a duty to leave room for people who disagree.

Section 35 covers the press and is one of the most detailed sections in the chapter:
- media professionals are free to report and comment within professional ethics;
- no outlet may be closed down in order to curb that freedom;
- no one may be forced to submit news to officials before publication, except in time of war;
- owners of newspapers and other media must be Thai nationals;
- the state may not subsidise private media with money or property;
- state agencies that pay media, for advertising, PR or anything similar, must report the details to the State Audit Commission and publish them;
- state officials who do media work have the same freedom, but must keep their agency's mission in mind.

Why so much about money? Because silencing the press does not require a padlock on the printing house. An advertising budget will do. An outlet that lives on a ministry's ad spending will find it harder to criticise that ministry. The disclosure rule turns an invisible string into a visible one.
